               <history>
                  <origin>
                     <origDate when-custom="108X/109X" notBefore="1080" notAfter="1099">s. xi ex.</origDate>
                  </origin>
                  <provenance type="#s"><orgName role="fmo" key="mlgbOrg_1">Exeter, Devon. Cathedral
                        church of St Peter</orgName><p>The 1506 catalogue records a 'textus' with
                        the second folio opening 'ad prudentiam iustorum'. The second leaf of this
                        manuscript begins 'prudentiam iustorum' (Luke 1:17; cf. BLR, xi (1985),
                        79-88).</p><p>The script resembles that of the latter part of MS. Bodl.
                        691.</p><note type="MLGB3_medievalCatalogueNotes">Cat. p.
                     350</note></provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">Scribblings, s. xvi, at fol. 65 and 68v
                     contain name of 'Robart Stokman of honytone' (ie Honiton, Devon) and at fol.
                     53v 'Robart Stockeman the younger'.</provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">John Coleridge, vicar and schoolmaster at
                     Ottery St Mary, gave it to William Coleridge, 19 December 1773.</provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">F. J. Coleridge, The Manor House, Ottery St
                     Mary, bought it from T. Kerslake, bookseller, Bristol, 1847.</provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">Edward Coleridge, Eton College.</provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">George Dunn, Woolley Hall, Maidenhead: his
                     sale, 2 February 1914, lot 837.</provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">A. N. L. Munby (1913-74), bought from Myles
                     Radford, 18 November 1946. Sale of Giles Munby, 22 June 1982, lot
                     41.</provenance>
               </history>
